\f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 
how is the floppy pen when you wiggle it loosely in between your fingers and a toy bow with a suction cup with a string thats not wound tight and the grip of the pen by the hand related
\par 09:31
\par These three phenomena are related through }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 standing wave mechanics}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 , }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 node/antinode physics}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 , and }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 rotational pivots}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 .
\par Here is exactly how these three specific examples connect:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 1. The Floppy Pen Illusion
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sa160\sl278\slmult1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls1\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid7951058 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 The Action:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 
 You pinch a rigid pen loosely at about one-third of its length and waggle your hand up and down.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 The Physics:}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058  Your fingers act as a loose, flexible pivot point (a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 node}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 
 of minimal displacement). The ends of the pen whip back and forth dynamically (the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 antinodes}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 
). Because the human eye cannot process the rapid changes in direction at the tips, your brain smooths out the motion. This creates the optical illusion that the rigid plastic is bending like rubber. [}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 HYPERLINK "https://www.penguinmagic.com/p/S5188"}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid7951058\charrsid7951058 {\*\datafield }}}{\fldrslt {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
